---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { person, website, graph, breadcrumb, PERSON_ID, SITE_ID, SITE_URL, LINKEDIN_URL } from '../lib/seo';

const jsonLd = graph(
  person,
  website,
  {
    '@type': 'ProfilePage',
    '@id': `${SITE_URL}/about/#webpage`,
    url: `${SITE_URL}/about/`,
    name: 'About Nick Manganiello',
    description:
      'How Nick Manganiello got here: early computers and DJing, DJN Event Productions and IT consulting at fourteen, then enterprise infrastructure architecture, theatrical production, facilities work and Stratora.',
    isPartOf: { '@id': SITE_ID },
    mainEntity: { '@id': PERSON_ID },
    about: { '@id': PERSON_ID },
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
  },
  breadcrumb([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITE_URL}/` },
    { name: 'About', url: `${SITE_URL}/about/` },
  ]),
);
---
<Base
  title="About Nick Manganiello — vNick.io"
  description="Nick Manganiello is an infrastructure architect on Long Island, New York. How I got here: computers at five, DJing at eight, DJN Event Productions and IT consulting at fourteen, then enterprise infrastructure, theatrical production, facilities work, and Stratora."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={jsonLd} />

  <main>
    <article class="page wrap">
      <h1 class="page-title">How I got here</h1>
      <p class="termline">nick@vnick.io:~$ whoami</p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I'm Nick Manganiello. You'll find Nicolas Manganiello on paperwork,
          but Nick is what I go by. I've spent most of my life building
          systems.
        </p>
        <p>
          I started experimenting with computers on Windows 95 around age five,
          built my first PC from spare parts at seven — it ran Windows 98 SE —
          and started DJing at eight. By fourteen I was operating DJN Event
          Productions and doing independent IT consulting for small businesses.
        </p>
        <p>
          From there the work expanded in a few directions at once: enterprise
          infrastructure, theatrical and event production, AV systems,
          construction, and facilities. They look like separate disciplines
          until you're standing in a server room, a theater, a manufacturing
          plant, or an offshore lighthouse and realize the same things matter
          every time — power, signal, networking, structure, redundancy, and
          making the whole system work when people actually need it.
        </p>

        <h2>Early systems</h2>
        <p>
          The computers came first. There was a family machine running Windows
          95, and by seven I had assembled my own from spare parts and gotten
          Windows 98 SE running on it. Taking things apart to see why they
          worked never really stopped; it just moved on to bigger things.
        </p>
        <p>
          The music gear came next. I started DJing at eight, and the early
          equipment was pieced together from the places a Long Island kid could
          actually get to — Music Trends in East Meadow, Sam Ash, the Guitar
          Center in Westbury. Learning signal flow on mixers and amplifiers
          turned out to be the same discipline as learning it on networks; I
          just didn't know that yet.
        </p>

        <h2>DJN and early consulting</h2>
        <p>
          At fourteen, two things started in the same year: DJN Event
          Productions, which I've owned and operated ever since, and
          independent IT consulting for small businesses.
        </p>
        <p>
          The consulting work was full-stack small-business infrastructure:
          network design and implementation, Windows Server 2003 through 2012,
          Active Directory and Small Business Server, storage, backup and
          disaster recovery, VoIP phone systems, Wi-Fi, structured cabling, and
          Office 365 migrations. When you're the only IT person a company has,
          you learn every layer, because there's nobody else to hand a problem
          to.
        </p>
        <p>
          DJN grew the same way. It started as DJ work and became a production
          company: live events, but also consulting and design for permanent
          installations at restaurants, bars, clubs, and catering halls — AV
          design, sound reinforcement, lighting and
          architectural lighting, control systems, networking, and integration.
          Along the way I've consulted for organizations including National
          Event Connection and The Suffolk Theater in Riverhead.
        </p>

        <h2>Theater and production</h2>
        <p>
          Around sixteen I started building theatrical sets, and that pulled a
          whole second trade into focus. Through Sanford H. Calhoun High
          School's theater program I learned set construction, lighting, and
          audio under Sal Salerno, the program's technical director. The same
          years brought framing, electrical, finish carpentry, trim and
          cabinetry, low-voltage and holiday lighting design — the
          physical-world skills that later turned out to matter as much in
          data centers as they do on stages.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other door into production was the Davidson family. Donald
          Davidson of HDH Davidson was a longtime family friend and a
          professional event-production mentor; I worked productions and jobs
          under him for years. His son Harley is my best friend — he grew up in
          production, brought me into Calhoun's theater program and into his
          father's work, and we've built Bellmore-Merrick school district
          productions and events together. A significant amount of what I know
          about theatrical set design, production systems, and FOH sound, I
          learned from Harley.
        </p>

        <h2>Enterprise infrastructure</h2>
        <p>
          The small-business consulting grew into enterprise work in 2014,
          when I joined CHIPS Technology Group as a senior network engineer
          and solutions architect. I did the pre-sales architecture —
          discovery, solution design, migration plans, proposals, RFP
          responses, SOWs — and then kept implementation ownership of what I
          designed and sold. That's the fastest honesty mechanism in this
          business: you don't over-promise a design you know you'll be racking
          yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
          Seven years there added up: infrastructure designed and implemented
          across more than three hundred customer offices; Citrix and
          Microsoft RDS platforms architected for five-thousand-plus users on
          hundreds of servers; a multi-tenant VMware data center with a
          dedicated virtual firewall, routing and VPN stack per customer; and
          technical integration for more than fifty mergers and acquisitions
          spanning a hundred-plus sites and companies — Active Directory and
          identity, email, endpoints, networks, backup, and the site cutovers
          that make an acquisition real.
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2021 I moved to Leviton, where the work grew into principal-level
          infrastructure architecture for a global manufacturer: reference
          designs, technical standards and platform roadmaps across cloud,
          networking, virtualization, compute, storage, backup and disaster
          recovery, endpoint management, identity, wireless and security —
          plus architectural direction for globally distributed engineers,
          vendor and technology selection, and the escalations that come with
          being the technical authority on cross-domain problems.
        </p>
        <p>
          The flagship was cloud. I designed, built and migrated roughly four
          hundred production workloads into Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and
          Oracle Cloud VMware Solution, owning the target architecture across
          OCVS, OCI networking, HCX, NSX, Palo Alto security, storage, backup
          and DR — and built a self-testing disaster recovery data center
          spanning multiple OCI regions. The migration ran on waves,
          dependency maps, runbooks and rollback plans, with more than
          seventy-five application and IT people on the cutover, testing and
          acceptance calls.
        </p>
        <p>
          Around it, the platform work: VMware transformed from five sites
          into a standardized environment across twenty-five-plus locations,
          dozens of clusters and some ninety hosts. A Veeam redesign across
          every site, backed by a dedicated replication data center, a
          continent-wide North American DR data center, and
          ransomware-resilient recovery — immutability, administrative
          separation, and automated recovery testing. A WAN migration off
          legacy MPLS onto Meraki SD-WAN, ClearPass NAC behind it, and IT/OT
          segmentation across manufacturing floors. And the endpoint layer:
          Intune and Autopilot architecture for three-thousand-plus Windows
          endpoints with certificate-based authentication over SCEP and Cloud
          PKI, alongside thousands of mobile devices and rugged scanners in
          plants and warehouses.
        </p>

        <h2>Building facilities, not just racks</h2>
        <p>
          A lot of infrastructure work assumes the room already exists. Mine
          usually hasn't. At CHIPS I designed and built more than two hundred
          offices and facilities from bare walls — healthcare, finance, legal,
          manufacturing, warehousing and logistics, engineering — owning the
          delivery from electrical requirements, HVAC load and UPS sizing
          through MDF/IDF design, structured cabling and fiber, predictive
          Wi-Fi design and RF surveys in Ekahau, AV and conference rooms, and
          the electricians, HVAC techs, low-voltage crews and general
          contractors it takes to land all of it on opening day.
        </p>
        <p>
          At Leviton the same work continues at manufacturer scale: sales
          offices, LIVE centers, plant expansions, relocations and acquired
          sites, from construction drawings through commissioning and handoff.
          The theatrical construction background does more work here than any
          certification: I can read the building, not just the rack elevation.
        </p>

        <h2>The lighthouse</h2>
        <p>
          For more than a decade I've worked with Harley and the HDH Davidson
          crew on the Huntington Lighthouse Preservation Society's Labor Day
          weekend Music Fest — a rock concert staged on an operating lighthouse
          in the middle of Huntington Bay. Harley leads the production; my
          areas are lighting design, programming and operation, video
          deployment, and the production networking, and I work cabling,
          rigging, power distribution, and load-in/load-out alongside the rest
          of the crew.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's a serious production in an environment that offers you nothing:
          Meyer Sound line arrays with distributed fills, professional FOH
          audio, architectural lighting on the lighthouse itself, moving heads,
          multichannel recording, video production, and livestreams to multiple
          platforms — all running on temporary LAN and WAN infrastructure with
          cellular uplinks, designed and built onsite, over water, every year.
          If you want to know why this site treats networking and lighting as
          one subject, the lighthouse is the answer.
        </p>

        <h2>Stratora</h2>
        <p>
          I'm the founder of <a href="https://stratora.io">Stratora</a> —
          self-hosted infrastructure monitoring and operational visibility for
          real-world IT and OT environments. It grew out of years of operating
          exactly that kind of mixed infrastructure and wanting better eyes on
          it. It has its own site, so I'll keep it short here.
        </p>

        <h2>What I'm doing now</h2>
        <p>
          Principal-level infrastructure architecture; running DJN; building
          Stratora; the lighthouse every Labor Day weekend; and writing things
          up here as I go. The writing is the part I've neglected the longest —
          this site is the fix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Away from the racks, I'm happily married to my wife Gina, we have
          one son, Luke, and there's my little buddy Rocco — a wheaten
          terrier.
        </p>

        <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
        <p>
          The writing lives at <a href="/writing">/writing</a> and selected
          builds are on the <a href="/projects">projects</a> page. Otherwise
          I'm on <a href={LINKEDIN_URL} rel="me">LinkedIn</a> and <a href="https://github.com/DJzmoot" rel="me">GitHub</a>,
          and the companies are <a href="https://stratora.io">Stratora</a> and <a href="https://djneventproductions.com/">DJN Event Productions</a>.
          Mail reaches me at <span class="term">nick@vnick.io</span>.
        </p>

      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
